# Q 1 — Quarto Dashboards vs. Shiny {background-color="#0a2540" transition="zoom"}

## Prompt

> *In [Dr. Albert Rapp's video](https://youtu.be/NjshpCevwQQ?si=alHUO4s2i6VQFoa5), he showed you how to create an interactive dashboard with Quarto. What did you like about the Quarto-based Interactive dashboard compared with other options introduced in this class previously (Shiny)?*

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## My Response — Overview {.scrollable}

::::::: columns
:::: {.column width="50%"}
### Quarto Dashboard

::: incremental
-   **No server required** for static dashboards — pure HTML output
-   Single `.qmd` source file; no `ui.R` + `server.R` split
-   Works with **R, Python, Julia, and Observable** in the same doc
-   Layouts defined with simple markdown fences (`::: {.card}`)
-   Deploy anywhere static files are served (GitHub Pages, Netlify, S3)
-   Theming via Bootswatch or custom SCSS — same system as presentations
:::
::::

:::: {.column width="50%"}
### Shiny

::: incremental
-   **Server-side reactivity** — full R process needed at runtime
-   Requires Shinyapps.io, Posit Connect, or self-hosted infra
-   Steeper learning curve (reactive expressions, observers, `req()`)
-   More powerful for **fully dynamic, user-driven** analyses
-   Large ecosystem (`shinydashboard`, `bslib`, `htmlwidgets`)
-   Better for complex multi-user apps with heavy computation
:::
::::
:::::::

::: aside
Both tools originate from Posit (formerly RStudio) and integrate tightly with the tidyverse.
:::

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## My Response — What I Liked Most

::: {.fragment .fade-in}
### 1 · Low Deployment Friction

Because a Quarto dashboard with `format: dashboard` outputs a **self-contained HTML file**, I can email it, commit it to GitHub, or drop it on a shared drive — no spinning up a server.\
In contrast, Shiny apps require Shinyapps.io credits or a Posit Connect license for anything beyond personal use.
:::

::: {.fragment .fade-in-then-semi-out}
### 2 · Unified Authoring Environment

I write the narrative, code, visualizations, and layout *in one file*.\
With Shiny I always felt I was context-switching between the "report writer" hat and the "app developer" hat.
:::

::: {.fragment .fade-up}
### 3 · Polyglot Support

Our capstone team uses both R (`lme4`, `quanteda`) and Python (`openai`).\
Quarto lets me embed both engines in the same dashboard — Shiny's Python bridge (`py-shiny`) is still maturing.
:::

::: notes
This slide demonstrates **fragments** with different animation types: `fade-in`, `fade-in-then-semi-out`, and `fade-up`.\
Each block appears independently on a click or arrow-press, which keeps the audience focused on one idea at a time.
:::

# Q 2 — Parameterized Reporting {background-color="#0a2540" transition="convex"}

## Prompt

> *Briefly explain what parameterized reporting is and how it works. Do you see the application of it in your workflow in your current or past jobs (or future jobs) as a performance marketer?*

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## What Is Parameterized Reporting?

Parameterized reporting lets you write **one Quarto document** and render it multiple times with different inputs — without touching the source code.

. . .

Parameters are declared in the YAML header:

``` yaml
---
params:
  brand: "Brand A"
  date_range_start: "2024-01-01"
  date_range_end:   "2024-03-31"
  channel: "paid_search"
---
```

. . .

Inside the document, access them with `params$brand`, `params$date_range_start`, etc.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Application in Performance Marketing

:::::: columns
:::: {.column width="55%"}
**Weekly Client Reporting**\
As a performance marketer managing paid search and social across multiple brands, I would use parameterized reports to:

::: incremental
-   Generate a branded **paid-media dashboard** per client with one render loop
-   Swap in the correct `date_range` for weekly vs. monthly cadence automatically
-   Send each client a PDF or HTML that *looks* hand-crafted but is fully automated
-   Add a `channel` parameter to split paid search, paid social, and display into separate sections
:::
::::

::: {.column width="40%"}
```{r}
#| label: param-table
#| echo: false
#| eval: true

# Illustrative parameter combinations a marketer might use
param_grid <- data.frame(
  Brand   = c("Brand A", "Brand B", "Brand C"),
  Channel = c("Paid Search", "Paid Social", "Display"),
  Period  = c("Weekly", "Monthly", "Monthly")
)

knitr::kable(param_grid,
             caption = "Example Parameter Grid",
             align   = "lll")
```
:::
::::::

[^1]

[^1]: Parameterized reporting is essentially the R equivalent of mail-merge — but for full data reports.

# Q 3 — Avoiding Dashboard Fatigue {background-color="#0a2540" transition="fade"}

## Prompt

> *In [Sean Nguyen's talk](https://youtu.be/fzykFa6c3LI?si=jOLPi3vtPTHKO4ld), "Beyond Dashboards: Dynamic Data Storytelling with Python, R, and Quarto Emails," what are his suggestions for making your dashboard effective and helping your colleagues pay attention to your report?*

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Key Suggestions from Sean Nguyen {.scrollable}

Sean Nguyen argues that static dashboards cause **fatigue** because they force the reader to do the interpretive work. His counter-strategy: *make the data come to the reader, not the other way around.*

. . .

### 1 · Push, Don't Pull

> "Send the insight, not the link."

Instead of expecting colleagues to navigate to a dashboard, **email the rendered report** (Quarto Email / `blastula`) directly to their inbox with the headline finding already visible in the preview pane.

. . .

### 2 · Dynamic Narrative Text

Replace static labels with **computed sentences**:

```{r}
#| echo: true
#| eval: false

# Example: dynamic inline text in a Quarto report
glue::glue(
  "Revenue grew by {scales::percent(growth_rate)} this week,
   driven by a {scales::percent(search_lift)} lift in paid search."
)
```

When the numbers update, the *sentence* updates — no manual copy-paste.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Key Suggestions (continued)

::::: columns
::: {.column width="48%"}
### 3 · Ruthless Prioritization

Show **one headline metric** prominently; bury everything else.\
Dashboard fatigue comes from *too much visible at once* — readers go numb.

### 4 · Conditional Formatting as Signal

Use color and icons not for aesthetics but as **alerts**:\
🟢 on target · 🟡 warning · 🔴 action required.\
The eye goes to color before text.
:::

::: {.column width="48%"}
### 5 · Scheduled, Automated Delivery

A report that arrives **every Monday at 8 AM** becomes a ritual.\
Random or manual distributions get ignored.\
Pair Quarto rendering with a cron job or GitHub Actions.

### 6 · Mobile-First Layout

Many stakeholders check reports on their phones first.\
Quarto's responsive HTML layouts (especially `format: dashboard`) handle this natively.
:::
:::::

