:::

# Q 4 — Challenges in Learning the Module Tools {background-color="#0a2540" transition="slide"}

## Prompt

> *Programming is all about increasing efficiency and reducing repetition. Share some of the challenges you might have in learning the various tools in the Module.*

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Challenges I Encountered {.scrollable}

### Challenge 1 · YAML Format

The Quarto YAML header controls everything — but a single misplaced space causes a silent failure or cryptic error.

::: {.fragment .fade-in}
``` yaml
# ✗ WRONG — colon without space breaks parsing
format:revealjs:

# ✓ CORRECT
format:
  revealjs:
    theme: default
```
:::

. . .

I learned to validate YAML with an online linter before rendering.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Challenges (continued)

### Challenge 2 · The Rendering Mental Model

Quarto renders in two passes: **knitr/Jupyter** executes the code, then **Pandoc** converts the markdown to the target format.\
Errors in either layer look different, and I initially mixed them up.

. . .

::: highlight-box
💡 **Rule of thumb:** If the error mentions `knitr`, look at your R code. If it mentions `pandoc`, look at your markdown/YAML.
:::

. . .

### Challenge 3 · CSS / SCSS Specificity Wars

Customizing the RevealJS theme requires overriding deeply nested CSS selectors. Using the wrong selector just does nothing — no error, no feedback. I had to rely heavily on browser DevTools to inspect the live slide and find the exact class names Quarto generates.

. . .

### Challenge 4 · Execution Caching

`freeze: auto` is wonderful for reproducibility but baffling when you first encounter stale output. Understanding *when* Quarto re-executes vs. reads from the `_freeze/` cache took experimentation.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Challenges (continued)

### Challenge 5 · Multi-Engine Projects

Our capstone uses both R and Python. Quarto supports both via `knitr::knit_engines$set()` or `jupyter`, but passing objects *between* engines in the same document is non-trivial. The `reticulate` bridge helps but adds its own complexity.

. . .

### Challenge 6 · Version Sprawl

Quarto, R, RStudio, `knitr`, `rmarkdown`, `tinytex` (for PDF), and the relevant packages all have their own version requirements. A fresh lab machine often requires 30+ minutes of setup before the first render succeeds.

::: {.fragment .highlight-box}
**Mitigation:** I now use `renv` to lock the R package environment and pin the Quarto version in a `.github/workflows` file for reproducible CI rendering.
:::

# Q 5 — Reflections on Quarto & AI {background-color="#0a2540" transition="concave"}

## Prompt

> *Share your thoughts about the learning experience you have had about Quarto, literate programming, and reproducible reports. Also, share your plan, if any, for using the knowledge in the era of AI.*

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Reflections on the Learning Journey

Quarto represents an interesting opportunity for data work.\
Before this module, my workflow was fragmented: run analysis in R → copy numbers → paste into PowerPoint → format manually.

::::::: columns
:::: {.column width="50%"}
**What Resonated**

::: incremental

-   The ability to produce a professional-looking HTML report, a PDF, and a RevealJS deck from the *same* `.qmd` source is genuinely remarkable
-   `freeze: auto` made long-running models practical in documents
:::
::::

:::: {.column width="50%"}
**What Surprised Me**

::: incremental

-   That Quarto can subsitute for paid platforms for reporting
-   The community around Quarto (extensions, themes, GitHub templates) is large and growing
:::
::::
:::::::

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Plan for the Era of AI

AI changes the equation in an interesting way: **AI can write code, but it cannot decide what story your data needs to tell.**

. . .

My plan has three layers:

::: {.fragment .fade-up}
**Layer 1 — AI as Code Accelerator**\
Use LLMs (Claude, Copilot) to scaffold the R code inside `.qmd` files — data wrangling, modeling, visualization. This cuts boilerplate time dramatically and lets me focus on interpretation.
:::

::: {.fragment .fade-up}
**Layer 2 — Quarto as the Trustworthy Shell**\
AI-generated code still needs to run, be versioned, and be auditable. Quarto provides that shell: the code is visible, the output is reproducible, and the narrative explains *why* decisions were made — something an AI prompt history does not preserve.
:::
